WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
MolRSI: recursive self-improvement for molecular discovery.
15.9% antibacterial hit rate. 8 selective pMHC binder hits.

00:06.000 --> 00:10.000
The inner loop optimizes workflows within the current task.

00:10.000 --> 00:15.500
The outer loop carries experience across tasks.
Both loops update shared memory.

00:15.500 --> 00:30.000
Accumulated experience improves the effective search policy:
which workflows to select, refine and repair next.

00:30.000 --> 00:33.500
More experience makes better workflows available for transfer.

00:33.500 --> 00:37.000
Antibacterial discovery: MolRSI compared with five agents
across all six antibacterial tasks.

00:37.000 --> 00:39.000
Experimental hit rate: 15.9% versus 8.7%
for an expert-designed, non-agentic workflow.

00:39.000 --> 00:42.500
Dose–response follow-up: selected compounds C3 and C32.
MRSA MICs of 1 and 8 micrograms per milliliter.

00:42.500 --> 00:47.500
De novo pMHC binder design: 8 selective binder hits
versus 0 for Claude Science under the same criteria.

00:47.500 --> 00:52.000
Two rounds of phage display and sequencing measure
target enrichment and preference over tested off-targets.

00:52.000 --> 00:58.000
Predicted on-target and off-target complexes.
Rotation and close-up of the binding interfaces.

00:58.000 --> 01:00.000
MolRSI. Experience improves the search policy.
